yupiter news
===============

1.0.2
-----

Released 23 October 2026.

This patch keeps the C17 interface, the C++23 implementation and the
ABI unchanged.

Build
~~~~~
  * "make install" refreshes the dynamic linker cache with ldconfig
    when the shared object is installed into the running system, so a
    program linked with "-lyupiter" finds libyupiter.so.1 without
    LD_LIBRARY_PATH.  A staged installation with DESTDIR leaves the
    cache to the package manager.  The program is "ldconfig" by
    default; "make install LDCONFIG=..." selects another one, and an
    empty LDCONFIG disables the refresh.  "make uninstall" refreshes
    the cache again after removing the files.
  * The shared object is libyupiter.so.1.0.2; its SONAME remains
    libyupiter.so.1.


1.0.1
-----

Released 22 October 2026.

This patch keeps the C17 interface, the C++23 implementation and the
ABI unchanged.

Build
~~~~~
  * ./configure recognizes GNU C++ when the compiler driver is invoked
    as "c++" and its --version banner names the distribution rather
    than GCC.  The compiler family is read from the driver's own -v
    output, while --version remains part of the error message.
  * test/check-freestanding.sh accepts the linker-defined
    "_GLOBAL_OFFSET_TABLE_" symbol emitted by x86_64 GCC position-
    independent code.  It is resolved by the linker and is not a C
    library or C++ runtime dependency.
  * The shared object is libyupiter.so.1.0.1; its SONAME remains
    libyupiter.so.1.


1.0.0
-----

Released 20 October 2026.

This is the first stable release of yupiter, the project formerly
released as libstdoc.  The rename is complete: the installed headers
are <yupiter.h> and <yupiter.hpp>, every exported symbol begins with
"yupiter_", every macro with "YUPITER_", the C++ namespace is
"yupiter", and the libraries are libyupiter.a and libyupiter.so.  The
version restarts at 1.0.0 and the shared object has SOVERSION 1.

The shape of the library is unchanged: the public interface is ISO
C17, the implementation is ISO C++23 compiled freestanding, a program
written in C and a program written in C++ link the same archive, and
the static archive still supplies its own entry point without a C
library or a C++ runtime.  Within the 1.x series the exported symbols,
their signatures, their C linkage and the public structure layouts are
stable; a change that breaks them requires a new major version.

The entries below this one are the history of libstdoc and keep the
names, symbol prefixes and version numbers with which they were
released.

Library
~~~~~~~

  * The allocator gains yupiter_realloc and yupiter_realloc_safe.
    yupiter_realloc resizes a live block, growing or shrinking it in
    place when the surrounding free space allows and moving it only
    when it cannot; a null pointer allocates and a size of 0 releases,
    as C17 permits.  The safe variant checks "count * size" for
    overflow and terminates with the documented status 134 on an
    invalid pointer, an overflowing size or an exhausted heap.  Added
    bytes of a growing resize are not initialised, and the public
    contract says so.
  * The three-argument system-call trampoline lives in
    assembly/yupiter_syscall3_aarch64.S and
    assembly/yupiter_syscall3_x86_64.S, while the six-argument form
    stays in the other trampoline files.  The split keeps the calls the
    platform layer actually makes free of the extra argument moves and
    of the stack argument on x86_64.
  * yupiter_stream_write bypasses the staging buffer when nothing is
    staged and either the stream is unbuffered or the payload is larger
    than YUPITER_BUFFER_SIZE, so a large buffered payload reaches the
    kernel in one call.

Interface
~~~~~~~~~

  * include/yupiter.h defines YUPITER_VERSION_MAJOR,
    YUPITER_VERSION_MINOR, YUPITER_VERSION_PATCH and
    YUPITER_VERSION_STRING, so a consumer can check at compile time
    which release it was built against.
  * The library is compiled with "-fvisibility=hidden" and the public
    declarations carry YUPITER_PUBLIC; the shared object therefore
    exports the documented C interface and nothing else.
  * include/yupiter.hpp remains the C++ entry point: it re-exposes the
    C17 interface and adds no symbol, type or runtime requirement.

Build
~~~~~

  * The CMake build is replaced by a GNU Makefile driven by a POSIX
    ./configure script.  ./configure detects Linux and the target
    architecture, validates that the compiler can build the freestanding
    C++23 implementation, and writes config.mk; make then builds both
    libraries by default.
  * libyupiter.a is the freestanding archive and includes the startup
    file.  libyupiter.so.1.0.0 is the hosted shared object: it excludes
    the startup file, has SONAME libyupiter.so.1, and names no C
    library or C++ runtime it needs.
  * The install rules provide pkg-config metadata and a hand-written
    CMake package configuration for consumers that use CMake.  Neither
    is needed to build the project, and no CMake is required on the
    build host.
  * The test harness is a set of POSIX shell scripts run by "make
    check"; CTest and the CMake test modules are gone.  The common
    warning list is spelled once per language, and warnings are errors.
  * Both libraries are built by default and can be selected separately
    with --enable-static and --enable-shared; the tests can be disabled
    with --enable-tests, and --build-type selects debug, release,
    relwithdebinfo or minsize.

Tests
~~~~~

  * short_write_retry replaces the three-argument trampoline for one
    test and scripts short, zero, oversized and failing write results,
    so the retry loop in yupiter_sys_write is exercised instead of only
    inspected.  A per-architecture rawsyscall helper forwards every
    unscripted call to the real kernel entry.
  * string_checks covers the null and zero-length cases of the internal
    length, copy and fill helpers.
  * The heap tests cover growth into the free tail, shrink with a
    split, an obstructed move, null and zero sizes, and the fatal
    failures of yupiter_realloc_safe.
  * The install test stages a DESTDIR installation and builds a
    consumer against the installed pkg-config metadata and, when CMake
    is available, the installed CMake package.

Packaging
~~~~~~~~~

  * Installed files: include/yupiter.h, include/yupiter.hpp,
    lib/libyupiter.a, lib/libyupiter.so.1.0.0 with the .so.1 and .so
    links, lib/pkgconfig/yupiter.pc and lib/cmake/yupiter/.
  * Supported targets are Linux on aarch64 and Linux on x86_64.  Any
    other operating system or architecture is rejected by ./configure
    instead of being mapped onto an implementation that does not match
    it.


2.0.1-beta
----------

Released 19 October 2026.

This release keeps the ISO C17 public interface and the ISO C++23
implementation language unchanged.  It concentrates on small, low-risk
improvements to the Linux system-call layer, the stream output path and
the internal string helpers; every exported symbol keeps its name,
signature and C linkage.


Library
~~~~~~~

  * assembly/syscall_aarch64.S and assembly/syscall_x86_64.S now
    provide stdoc_syscall3 alongside the existing six-argument
    stdoc_syscall.  io/syscall_linux.cxx uses the three-argument entry
    point for read, write, close and exit_group, which is every call
    the platform layer wraps: aarch64 shifts three argument registers
    instead of six, and x86_64 also avoids the stack load that the
    sixth argument needed.  The kernel ABI and the portable code above
    the trampoline are unchanged, and the six-argument entry point
    remains for a future call that needs it.
  * assembly/start_aarch64.S tail-branches to stdoc_sys_exit instead of
    calling a function that never returns.  The stack stays 16-byte
    aligned and the link register still points into the startup file as
    a guard.  The x86_64 entry point keeps its call: after main returns,
    %rsp is 16-byte aligned, and the call gives stdoc_sys_exit the
    8-mod-16 alignment the System V AMD64 ABI gives a called function.
  * io/file.cxx: stdoc_stream_write bypasses the staging buffer when
    nothing is staged and either the stream is unbuffered or the
    payload is larger than STDOC_BUFFER_SIZE.  The unbuffered case no
    longer copies and flushes the payload first, and a large buffered
    payload is handed to stdoc_sys_write in one call instead of being
    copied through the buffer and flushed in 256-byte pieces.  On a
    buffered stream, a payload of exactly STDOC_BUFFER_SIZE bytes still
    fills the buffer, so the documented staging behaviour is preserved.
  * io/string.cxx: stdoc_memcpy and stdoc_memset move sixteen bytes at
    a time through a local byte block, which the compiler turns into
    wide loads and stores without a cast that would break alignment or
    aliasing rules.  The functions still read and write only inside the
    caller's length.  stdoc_strlen now walks with a pointer, but stays
    byte-wise on purpose: a word-at-a-time scan could read past the
    terminating null and outside the object.


Build
~~~~~

  * The package version is 2.0.1.  CMake versions carry no suffix, so
    the beta status of this release is recorded here in NEWS rather
    than in libstdoc.pc or the CMake package version.
  * A configure that does not choose CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE now selects
    RelWithDebInfo instead of leaving the build with no optimisation
    flags at all.  The default is recorded in the cache, so a later
    build in the same directory keeps it; Debug, Release and
    MinSizeRel are still chosen explicitly, and a multi-configuration
    generator continues to choose the type at build time.
  * The two target workflows now set the build type explicitly: the
    ordinary GNU C and Clang rows build RelWithDebInfo, and the
    smallest-arena row builds Debug.  Both an optimised and an
    unoptimised configuration therefore keep running the whole suite.
  * The two target workflows and the static-analysis job now run on
    Ubuntu 26.04 LTS: ubuntu-26.04 on x86_64 and ubuntu-26.04-arm on
    aarch64.  The two workflow files carry the release in their names
    and are renamed from -ubuntu-lts-24.04.yml to -ubuntu-lts-26.04.yml
    accordingly; docs/building and docs/repository follow.  The CodeQL
    workflow names ubuntu-26.04 instead of ubuntu-latest, which GitHub
    migrates to 26.04 during October and November 2026.
  * .github/workflows/x86_64-linux-gnu-ubuntu-lts-26.04.yml: the
    architecture guard gives every rejected processor a build
    directory of its own.  The loop used one directory for all of
    them, and a failed configure writes CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR into
    that directory's cache, so the second configure reported i686 -
    the processor the first iteration tried - instead of the one it
    was given, and the grep that requires the message to name that
    processor failed.  The job failed this way at 5e5d72a; it now
    passes for i686, arm, riscv64 and ppc64le.


Documentation
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  * docs/building documents the RelWithDebInfo default, how to choose
    another build type, and the explicit build types of the CI matrix.
  * docs/io describes the direct-write stream path, the two
    system-call trampolines, the aarch64 tail branch and the block
    copies in io/string.cxx; docs/formatting shows the direct path in
    the flow of one formatted call.
  * docs/testing and README were brought in line with the default
    build type, the stream test's larger-than-buffer case and the
    2.0.1-beta version.


2.0.0-beta
----------

Released 18 October 2026.

This is a major release.  The implementation moved from ISO C17 to ISO
C++23 and Linux on x86_64 joined Linux on aarch64, so what builds the
library changed: CMake 3.20 or newer, a C++23 compiler and a standard
library with freestanding mode.  The public interface is unchanged -
include/libstdoc.h is still ISO C17, every exported function keeps its
name, signature and C linkage, and every structure layout is the same -
so a program that links the archive needs no change.  This entry
records everything since the 1.0.0-beta release.


Bug fixes
~~~~~~~~~

  * io/syscall_linux.cxx: a system call that completed a transfer of 4096
    bytes or more was reported as a failure.  The raw kernel result was
    classified by comparing it, as an unsigned integer, against 4096 -
    the magnitude of the smallest kernel error rather than the error
    itself - so every count above that became "-1" and the bytes were
    silently treated as lost.  Nothing in the library wrote more than
    256 bytes at a time (the stream staging buffer is 256 bytes), so the
    defect was invisible from the public interface and reachable only
    through stdoc_sys_write.  The comparison is now against -4095, which
    puts every failure in the top 4095 values of the unsigned range and
    leaves every count below it.  test/largewrite.c pins the boundary.
  * test/heap.c: the printf-scratch scenario used a format string whose
    meaning is not the same everywhere - "%08.4d" combines the '0' flag
    with a precision, and the standard says a precision makes the flag
    inert - and passed a void pointer to %s.  It now uses conversions
    whose meaning this dialect defines and a null char pointer, since
    what that case exercises is the scratch accounting, not those
    corners.

Library
~~~~~~~

  * mem/heap.cxx: a release now merges the free neighbours in the single
    walk that finds the block, instead of walking the arena a second
    time.  The list keeps one invariant - no two adjacent blocks are
    free - which makes the merge a local operation; a release is
    therefore roughly a third faster in a loop of allocations and
    releases over a full arena, and the walk validates both neighbours
    before it writes anything.
  * io/file.cxx: stdoc_stream_write copies payloads into the staging
    buffer block by block and scans only the newly staged bytes for a
    newline, so a large write no longer copies the same bytes twice.
  * io/vfprintf.cxx: the padding chunk is filled once per call instead of
    once per character.  "%p" now honours the '+' and ' ' flags the way
    the reference C library does, printing "+0x1234" and " 0x1234" for
    a non-null pointer and holding no sign for "(nil)".
  * include/libstdoc.h: stdoc_printf and stdoc_fprintf are declared
    with the GNU "format(printf)" attribute where the compiler
    understands it, so a mismatched call is caught while the program is
    built.  The header documents that "%+p" is accepted by the
    functions while "-Wformat" reports it, and how to quiet that one
    diagnostic.
  * include/stdoc_io.hxx: stdoc_sys_read and stdoc_sys_close are
    documented as the platform layer of the input release that
    stdoc_stdin is waiting for, rather than left looking like dead
    code.
  * The implementation moved from ISO C17 to ISO C++23.  Every file
    under io/ and mem/ is now a .cxx translation unit built as C++23
    inside the stdoc namespace, with scoped enumerations, "constexpr"
    and "static_assert" in place of macros and a "constinit" guarantee
    on the stream objects and the heap arena: an object that would need
    dynamic initialization is rejected while the library is compiled,
    which is exactly what the freestanding entry point requires, since
    it runs no constructors and walks no ".init_array".  The public
    interface is unchanged: include/libstdoc.h is still ISO C17, every
    exported function keeps its name, signature and C linkage, and
    every structure layout is the same.
  * include/stdoc_io.hxx and include/stdoc_mem.hxx replace the internal
    headers io/stdoc_io.h and mem/stdoc_mem.h.  They are still ISO C17
    on purpose - the C tests include them - they guard their
    declarations with "extern "C"", and they are not installed.
  * The library implements Linux on x86_64 as well as Linux on
    aarch64.  The new assembly/start_x86_64.S is the program entry
    point of the target: it loads argc, argv and envp, aligns the stack
    to a multiple of 16 bytes before the call, as the System V AMD64
    ABI requires, and hands main's return value to stdoc_sys_exit.  The
    new assembly/syscall_x86_64.S is the system-call trampoline, which
    permutes the argument registers into the kernel's convention
    (number in rax, fourth argument in r10 because "syscall" destroys
    rcx, sixth argument on the stack).  io/syscall_linux.cxx chooses
    the system-call numbers of the architecture the compiler targets -
    close 3, read 0, write 1 and exit_group 231 on x86_64, alongside
    the numbers already there for aarch64 - and refuses an
    architecture without an entry instead of mapping it onto another
    one's numbers.  The aarch64 trampoline moved from assembly/syscall.S
    to assembly/syscall_aarch64.S, so the two files a target provides
    are named the way assembly/start_aarch64.S already was.  The
    public interface is untouched: the entry point still calls the
    unmangled "main" symbol, and both targets export the same symbols.


Build
~~~~~

  * The warning set grew from -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror to
    include -Wconversion, -Wsign-conversion, -Wshadow,
    -Wstrict-prototypes, -Wmissing-prototypes, -Wcast-qual, -Wundef,
    -Wswitch-default, -Wdouble-promotion and -Wvla.  Every file in the
    project, tests included, builds clean under both GNU C and Clang
    with it.
  * "-no-pie" is gone from the link options.  "-static" already
    produces a non-PIE executable, and passing "-no-pie" as well only
    made Clang report it as an unused argument on every link.
  * STDOC_HEAP_SIZE is validated when the project is configured: a
    value that is not a plain decimal number, or that is below the 64
    bytes the allocator can actually work with, is rejected with a
    message instead of failing later.  The value also reaches the test
    programs now, so a test is never built against a different arena
    from the library it links.
  * A new CI workflow
    (.github/workflows/aarch64-linux-gnu-ubuntu-lts-24.04.yml) builds
    and tests under GNU C and Clang on an aarch64 runner, at the
    default arena size and at the smallest one accepted, regenerates
    the format golden file from the host C library and compares it,
    and checks that the configuration guard rejects the values it
    documents as impossible.
  * The architecture guard accepts x86_64 - and the amd64 and AMD64
    spellings the same architecture arrives under - as well as aarch64
    and its arm64 and ARM64 spellings.  The processor it selects names
    the startup file and the system-call trampoline the archive is
    built from, so a target that is not implemented still fails the
    configure with a message instead of being given another one's
    implementation.
  * CMake 3.20 or newer is required - the first release that knows
    C++23 - and the C++ compiler is no longer optional: the project
    asks for the standard with target_compile_features and rejects a
    compiler that does not provide it.  A configure-time probe compiles
    the freestanding C++ headers the implementation includes
    (<climits>, <cstdarg>, <cstddef>, <cstdint> under -ffreestanding
    -fno-exceptions -fno-rtti) and fails with a message that names what
    is missing; libstdc++ provides them from GCC 13 on, while libc++
    and the MSVC standard library do not implement freestanding mode.
  * The library is built with the C++ warning set - the C set minus
    -Wstrict-prototypes and -Wmissing-prototypes, plus -Wold-style-cast,
    -Wnon-virtual-dtor, -Woverloaded-virtual and
    -Wzero-as-null-pointer-constant - and with -fno-exceptions,
    -fno-rtti and -fno-threadsafe-statics added to the freestanding
    flags it already used.  The C test programs keep the C set.
  * The clang-tidy job checks the C++ implementation files as C++23 and
    the remaining C files as C17, each with the warning flags of its
    language.
  * The x86_64 workflow
    (.github/workflows/x86_64-linux-gnu-ubuntu-lts-24.04.yml) builds,
    tests and verifies the x86_64 target on the x86_64 runner.  It
    used to cross-compile aarch64 and run the suite through
    qemu-user; now that both targets are implemented, each workflow
    builds the architecture of its own runner, so the cross toolchain,
    the emulator and the binfmt_misc registration are gone.  Its
    architecture-guard job now refuses a configure for a processor
    that is not implemented, and accepts the alias spellings of the
    two that are, instead of refusing the host it runs on.


Tests
~~~~~

  * Fourteen tests under CTest, up from nine.
  * test/differential.c compiles the same source once against the host C
    library and once against libstdoc and compares the two case by case
    - over two thousand combinations of flags, width and precision,
    built at run time.  Unlike a golden file, the reference is live.
  * test/stream.c covers the staging buffer, the discard and the
    relative order of stdout and stderr, compared against golden output
    in which the two streams are merged.
  * test/largewrite.c writes 200000 bytes through the system-call
    wrapper and checks the status, the length and the bytes on a file
    and through a pipe.
  * install_and_consume installs the library into a scratch prefix and
    builds and runs test/consumer against it with find_package.
  * test/heap.c and test/heap_abort.c shape their scenarios from
    stdoc_heap_capacity instead of assuming an arena of a particular
    size, so they pass from 64 bytes upwards.
  * A new archive_is_freestanding test inspects the built archive: every
    undefined symbol has to be "main" or a "stdoc"-prefixed symbol of
    the library, no exported symbol may carry a C++ mangled name, and
    no member may contain an ".init_array", ".ctors", ".tdata" or
    similar section.  A static library is never linked on its own, so
    this is what proves the freestanding property for every member
    instead of only for the members a particular link happens to need.
  * The C++ compatibility test is still compiled as C++17, and it is
    now always built: a C++ compiler is part of the required
    toolchain.


Documentation
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  * docs/testing, docs/building, docs/formatting, docs/io and docs/heap
    were brought back in line with the code: the arena minimum, the
    warning set, the link options, the count-versus-error boundary, the
    one-pass release, the compile-time format check, and the new tests.
  * docs/building records a detail of linking without a C runtime that
    is easy to meet the hard way: a static executable whose read-write
    segment has no file-backed content is placed at a file offset past
    the end of the file and dies with SIGBUS before main, and one
    initialized object prevents it.
  * docs/testing states what is still not covered, including the retry
    for a short write and any architecture or operating system other
    than the one the project is built for.
  * docs/repository, docs/building, docs/testing, docs/io, docs/heap
    and docs/formatting record the new file names and the C++23
    implementation, and docs/building states the toolchain the project
    now needs: CMake 3.20 or newer, a C++23 compiler and a standard
    library that supports freestanding mode.
  * README, docs/building, docs/testing, docs/io, docs/repository and
    the installed pkg-config description now name both targets, and
    describe the two entry points, the two system-call trampolines and
    the two CI workflows instead of the single aarch64 implementation.


Version
~~~~~~~

  * The package version is 2.0.0.  CMake versions carry no suffix, so
    the beta status of this release is recorded here in NEWS rather
    than in libstdoc.pc or the CMake package version.


1.0.0-beta
--------------

Released 14 October 2026.

This entry records everything from the commit "major changes"
(fbc80cd), which introduced the present implementation, through the
automation commits that followed (92a8928, dependabot.yml; 79eefdc,
the CodeQL workflow), up to the state of the tree at this release,
including the dynamic memory allocator and the documentation.


Build and project structure
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  * New CMake build (CMake 3.16 or newer) producing a static archive,
    with strict warnings (-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror) for every
    file written for this project and ISO C17 with extensions off for
    the whole tree, tests included.
  * Freestanding by design: the library is built without a host C
    library (-ffreestanding -fno-builtin -fno-stack-protector) and the
    tests link with -nostdlib -static -no-pie, so the library's own
    entry point, printf and string helpers are the only ones a test
    program can use.
  * Platform selection at configure time: Linux on aarch64 is
    supported, anything else fails with a message that names what is
    missing instead of silently picking an incompatible
    implementation.
  * New directory layout: assembly/ (runtime), io/ (streams,
    formatting, system calls), mem/ (allocator), include/ (public
    API), etc/ (package metadata), test/ (tests), docs/
    (documentation).
  * Installation of the archive, the headers, a CMake package
    (find_package(libstdoc)) and a pkg-config file.
  * Multi-licensing restructured: LICENSE explains the choice and
    LICENSES/ carries the full texts of GPLv2+, LGPLv2.1+ and
    Apache-2.0 together with a README on how the choice works.
  * README rewritten around the new structure.


Library
~~~~~~~

  * stdoc_printf and stdoc_fprintf, backed by a new formatting engine
    (stdoc_vfprintf): the conversions %d %i %u %o %x %X %c %s %p and
    %%, the flags '-', '+', ' ', '#' and '0', a numeric or '*' width,
    a '.' precision (numeric or '*'), and the length modifiers hh, h,
    l, ll, z, t and j.
  * The documented failure contract: a width or precision literal
    that does not fit in an int, a field that would not fit in the
    return value and a rejected write all fail the call with -1, a
    failed call discards what it had staged, and a stream error is
    sticky.
  * Standard streams: stdoc_stdout is buffered and flushes on a
    newline and at the end of every completed call, stdoc_stderr is
    unbuffered so ordering is preserved, stdoc_stdin is reserved for a
    future input release.
  * Operating-system layer isolated in io/syscall_linux.c (the only
    place with system-call numbers), a generic system-call trampoline
    in assembly/syscall.S and the program entry point in
    assembly/start_aarch64.S - no constructors and no atexit handling,
    which is why the formatted functions flush themselves.
  * Internal stdoc_strlen, stdoc_memcpy and stdoc_memset so the
    library links with -nostdlib without taking symbols from a host
    C library.
  * Public headers: include/libstdoc.h (ISO C17, freestanding-safe)
    and include/libstdoc.hpp (extern "C" linkage for C++ consumers).


Dynamic memory
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  * stdoc_malloc and stdoc_free over a static arena of 4096 bytes that
    never asks the operating system for memory, so a leak is bounded
    by the arena size and can be counted.  First-fit allocation with
    block splitting, and merging of adjacent free blocks on release,
    so the arena returns to one block when everything is freed.  The
    size is configurable with -DSTDOC_HEAP_SIZE=<bytes>.
  * The safe variants: stdoc_malloc_safe checks "count * size" for
    overflow, zeroes what it returns and terminates the process with a
    diagnostic on failure; stdoc_free_safe rejects pointers outside
    the heap, interior pointers, double frees and corrupted headers
    the same way.  Both exit with status 134.
  * Accounting of live bytes and live blocks, which the tests assert
    to be zero after every scenario.
  * The formatting engine now borrows its digit and padding scratch
    buffer (97 bytes) from the heap for the duration of a call and
    falls back to stack buffers when the heap is exhausted, so running
    out of heap never makes the formatted functions fail.


Tests
~~~~~

  * Nine tests under CTest: golden output for index.c and format.c,
    the same golden output from C++, the failure paths of the
    formatted functions, the heap and its leak accounting, and the
    four fail-fast scenarios of the safe allocator, each of which has
    to end with the documented fatal status 134.
  * The golden file of test/format.c was produced by a reference C
    library, so the comparison checks the dialect and not the
    implementation's habits.


Documentation
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  * New docs/ directory documenting the whole repository in plain
    text: docs/README (the index and reading order), docs/repository
    (every top-level entry), docs/building, docs/testing,
    docs/formatting, docs/io and docs/heap.  README points at it.


Continuous integration
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  * .github/dependabot.yml keeps the GitHub Actions dependencies
    updated (92a8928), and .github/workflows/codeql.yml runs CodeQL
    analysis (79eefdc).  Neither file takes part in the build: the
    project builds with no network access.


Version
~~~~~~~

  * The package version is 1.0.0.  CMake versions carry no suffix, so
    the beta status of this release is recorded here in NEWS rather
    than in libstdoc.pc or the CMake package version.
